# Codex tool mapping for pstack

pstack skills are written in Claude Code tool language (the `Skill` tool, the `Agent` tool, `AskUserQuestion`, Claude model names). On Codex the skills are the same files; only the tool names resolve differently. Read this when a pstack skill names a Claude tool, a driver or bundled skill, or a Claude model. This file is Codex-specific. Gemini CLI, opencode, Prime Agent, and other runtimes must use their own concrete tools, model names, and configuration paths.

## Tool actions

| pstack / Claude action | Codex equivalent |
|------------------------|------------------|
| Read a file | `shell` (`cat`, `head`, `tail`) |
| Create / edit / delete a file | `apply_patch` |
| Run a shell command | `shell` |
| Search file contents / find files | `shell` (`rg`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`) |
| Fetch a URL | `shell` with `curl` / `wget` |
| Search the web | `web_search` |
| Invoke a skill (the `Skill` tool, `/command`) | Skills load natively. Follow the instructions presented. |
| Dispatch a subagent (the `Agent`/`Task` tool) | `spawn_agent` |
| Dispatch N parallel subagents in one turn | N `spawn_agent` calls in one response |
| Wait for a subagent result | `wait_agent` |
| Free a finished subagent slot | `close_agent`, when the session exposes it. Some Codex hosts don't. |
| Track tasks (the todolist; `TaskCreate` / `TaskUpdate`, or `TodoWrite` on Claude Code) | `update_plan` |
| Ask the human a fixed-choice question (`AskUserQuestion`) | Ask in plain text and let the user answer. Codex has no structured-choice tool. |

Subagent dispatch needs `multi_agent` enabled. Add to `~/.codex/config.toml`:

```toml
[features]
multi_agent = true
```

Without it, `spawn_agent` is unavailable and the fan-out skills (`interrogate`, `why`, `how`, `arena`, `reflect`) degrade to a single sequential pass. A required independent review does not degrade. It stays blocked, as poteto-mode's [Subagents](../SKILL.md#subagents) section says.

Enabling `multi_agent` does not lift the host's thread limit. A thread-limit error from `spawn_agent` is a capacity error, not a rejected model slug, so the skills' fallback to another model does not apply. After the first capacity rejection, stop spawning. Close a finished agent with `close_agent` before you try again, and where the session has no `close_agent`, do not assume a finished agent's slot is free.

## Subagent policy

poteto-mode's Subagents section sets Claude-specific defaults (`subagent_type: "pstack:poteto-agent"`, `run_in_background: true`). On Codex:

- There is no `poteto-agent` subagent type. Route an ad-hoc subagent through poteto-mode's style by dispatching a `spawn_agent` whose instructions tell it to read the `poteto-mode` skill in full first.
- `spawn_agent` calls already run concurrently with your turn, so `run_in_background: true` has no separate flag. Issue the dispatch and continue.
- There are no `pstack:effort-<level>` or `pstack:poteto-agent-<level>` types. When a role value carries `@<level>`, or the `default effort` line names a level, pass that level as `spawn_agent`'s `reasoning_effort` and keep the dispatch otherwise unchanged. `session` passes no `reasoning_effort`.
- There is no `comment-sicko` subagent type either. The **no-comments** skill spawns it on Claude Code; on Codex dispatch a `spawn_agent` whose instructions tell it to read `poteto-mode/references/agents/comment-sicko.md` in full first.
- Claude Code runs every subagent on this machine, so the **swarm** skill's workers and the fan-out playbooks (`orchestrate`, `autopilot-full`, `autopilot-stack`) isolate writers with worktrees. The same holds on Codex.
- Keep the rest of the policy unchanged. Pass file pointers not inlined context, give each worker its own worktree or branch when they write, review every subagent's diff yourself.

## Model names

Skills name Claude defaults (a single-role default for code/prose/judgment plus a diverse-model panel for diverse-model panels; each model-consuming skill lists its own in a Models section). These slugs do not resolve on Codex. Substitute your configured Codex models:

- Single-model roles: your primary Codex model (for example `gpt-6-sol`).
- Roles that default to the strongest Claude model (`bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, `hillclimb`, `strongest judgment`): your strongest Codex model (for example `gpt-6-astra`).
- Diverse-model panels (`arena`, `architect`, `interrogate`, `how` critics, `reflect`): the adversarial signal comes from model diversity, so use the distinct Codex models available to you. A good default panel on ChatGPT is `gpt-6-astra`, `gpt-6-sol`, `gpt-6-luna`. If only one model family is reachable, vary reasoning effort and note in the verdict that diversity was reduced.

`/setup-pstack` writes the configured model list. On Codex, set it to your Codex model slugs.

## Session routing hook

The native pstack plugin bundles the same `SessionStart` routing instruction as the Claude Code plugin, through `session-start.sh` on macOS and Linux and `session-start.ps1` on Windows. Codex runs the hook on startup, resume, clear, and compact after the user trusts the hook through `/hooks`. The hook reads `session hook` from the Codex sheet, at the path in [setup-pstack's runtime table](../../setup-pstack/SKILL.md#other-runtimes); `session hook: off` disables injection.

A skills-only installation does not include plugin hooks. Request `poteto-mode` explicitly or add a standing instruction to `AGENTS.md` in that case.

## Driver and bundled skills pstack references

The [driver policy](../SKILL.md#non-negotiables) selects the app driver. For skills and drivers named by these workflows, use these Codex equivalents:

| Skill or driver named in pstack | On Codex |
|---------------------------------|----------|
| `run` (drive a CLI/TUI to see a change work) | Run the app yourself via `shell` and observe the real output. |
| Project UI driver | Drive the UI with whatever automation you have, or hand the user a concrete manual check. Do not claim done without observing the artifact. |
| `plugin-dev:skill-development` (Claude's SKILL.md authoring guidance) | Follow your platform's skill-authoring guidance; the `writing-skills` skill if present. Keep `name` + `description` frontmatter and progressive disclosure. |
| `loop` (recurring/self-paced re-invocation, used by `babysit`) | Codex has no `loop` skill. Re-run the step yourself on a cadence, or use a Codex scheduled task if available. |

## Per-skill notes

Affected skill entry points and the optional Codex slash stubs point here. Most skills need only the tables above. These need one more mapping:

| Skill | On Codex |
|-------|----------|
| `interrogate` | The `subagent_type`/`model`/`readonly` dispatch fields map to `spawn_agent`; substitute your configured Codex models and keep the reviewer panel model-diverse. |
| `setup-pstack` | The skill's Other runtimes table names the Codex sheet path and how it loads; the slugs are your Codex models (see Model names above). The role rows are identical. |
| `no-comments` | There is no `comment-sicko` subagent type; see Subagent policy above. |
| `teach` | Running `how` and `why` in parallel maps to `spawn_agent` fan-out; image generation uses the configured Codex equivalent. |
| `create-verification-skill` | The generated skill lands under `.claude/skills/verify/` on Claude Code; write it to Codex's project-skill location instead. The app-driving harness is platform-neutral. |
| `maintain-verification-skill` | The parallel per-feature source readers map to `spawn_agent` fan-out; the project-local skill lives under Codex's skills location, not `.claude/skills/`. |
| `babysit` | `loop` and `AskUserQuestion` resolve through the tables above. |
| `automate-me` | `plugin-dev:skill-development` resolves through the skills table above. |
| `architect` | The runner panel goes through the **arena** skill, so its `spawn_agent` fan-out and model substitution apply here too. |
| `arena` | The parallel candidates and the cross-judge map to `spawn_agent`; substitute your configured Codex models for the runners and the cross-judge pool (see Model names above). |
| `how` | The parallel explorers and the explainer map to `spawn_agent` fan-out; substitute your configured Codex models. |
| `reflect` | The three reviewers and the synthesizer map to `spawn_agent`; substitute your configured Codex models. The transcript finder reads Claude Code's layout under `~/.claude/projects/`, so pass the session digest step 1 allows instead. |
| `swarm` | Each worker is a `spawn_agent` call on your configured Codex model, and those calls already run concurrently; give each writing worker its own worktree or output directory (see Subagent policy above). |
| `why` | The parallel investigators and the synthesizer map to `spawn_agent`; substitute your configured Codex models. List MCP servers from the tools Codex exposes to the session, not from `.mcp.json` or `claude mcp list`. |

## Vendored scripts

`skills/poteto-mode/scripts/` ships the `watch-pr` PR watcher, the `orch` store CLI, and `worktree-audit.mjs`. The `watch-pr/ship-pr` command owns pending-merge inspection and cancellation; `resume.mjs` owns the shared checkpoint locator described in [Resume storage](resume-storage.md). These scripts use bun and Node.js and run the same on Codex; invoke them through `shell`. They need `bun`, `gh`, and (for stack work) `gt`. Run `worktree-audit.mjs` with `node`, as its shebang does. Under bun, a macOS cloud-storage link in your home directory fails to resolve, so every worktree under it loses its last chat and lands in `review`. `worktree-audit.mjs` scans Codex sessions under `$CODEX_HOME/sessions` and `$CODEX_HOME/archived_sessions` (default `~/.codex`), along with any Claude Code or Pi transcript directory that exists. It imports the transcript walker from `skills/reflect/scripts/find-transcript.mjs`, so keep the `reflect` skill installed beside `poteto-mode`.

## Instructions file

Where a pstack skill says "your instructions file", on Codex that is `AGENTS.md` (project root, plus `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` global). On Claude Code it is `CLAUDE.md`.
//! Funder groups and their direct evidence, built from the surviving stored identities.

use std::collections::{BTreeMap, BTreeSet};

use serde::Deserialize;
use thiserror::Error;
use uuid::Uuid;

use super::{
    entity_details::{FunderAliasLink, FunderFamilyLink},
    funder::FunderId,
};

/// Canonical name and recorded aliases for one persisted identity.
pub struct GroupingFunder {
    /// Persistent identity.
    pub id: FunderId,
    /// Canonical source name.
    pub name: String,
    /// Exact recorded spellings.
    pub aliases: Vec<String>,
}

/// Complete replacement for the stored group membership and evidence.
pub struct FunderGroups {
    /// Every input identity belongs to exactly one component.
    pub(crate) memberships: Vec<(FunderId, FunderId)>,
    /// Direct equal-name evidence, without inferred transitive links.
    pub(crate) alias_links: Vec<FunderAliasLink>,
    /// Direct reviewed-family evidence, kept separate from equal-name evidence.
    pub(crate) family_links: Vec<FunderFamilyLink>,
}

#[derive(Deserialize)]
#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
struct FamilyPolicy {
    version: PolicyVersion,
    families: Vec<ReviewedFamily>,
}

#[derive(Deserialize)]
#[serde(try_from = "u32")]
enum PolicyVersion {
    V1,
}

impl TryFrom<u32> for PolicyVersion {
    type Error = &'static str;

    fn try_from(version: u32) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {
        match version {
            1 => Ok(Self::V1),
            _ => Err("unsupported reviewed funder family policy version"),
        }
    }
}

#[derive(Deserialize)]
#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
struct ReviewedFamily {
    key: String,
    names: Vec<String>,
}

#[derive(Eq, PartialEq, Ord, PartialOrd)]
struct NormalizedName(String);

impl NormalizedName {
    fn parse(name: &str) -> Option<Self> {
        let name = name
            .split_whitespace()
            .collect::<Vec<_>>()
            .join(" ")
            .to_lowercase();
        (!name.is_empty()).then_some(Self(name))
    }
}

/// Invalid grouping input or reviewed policy.
#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum FunderGroupingError {
    /// The versioned resource does not match its schema.
    #[error("invalid reviewed funder policy: {0}")]
    PolicyDecode(#[from] serde_json::Error),
    /// Policy names and keys must identify one reviewed family.
    #[error("invalid reviewed funder policy: {0}")]
    Policy(String),
    /// A persisted identity must occur exactly once in a snapshot.
    #[error("duplicate funder identity {0}")]
    DuplicateFunder(Uuid),
}

impl FamilyPolicy {
    fn parse(input: &str) -> Result<Self, FunderGroupingError> {
        let policy: Self = serde_json::from_str(input)?;
        let mut keys = BTreeSet::new();
        let mut names = BTreeSet::new();
        for family in &policy.families {
            if family.key.trim().is_empty() || !keys.insert(&family.key) {
                return Err(FunderGroupingError::Policy(
                    "family keys must be nonempty and unique".into(),
                ));
            }
            if family.names.is_empty() {
                return Err(FunderGroupingError::Policy(
                    "a reviewed family must have names".into(),
                ));
            }
            for name in &family.names {
                let normalized = NormalizedName::parse(name).ok_or_else(|| {
                    FunderGroupingError::Policy("reviewed names must be nonempty".into())
                })?;
                if !names.insert(normalized) {
                    return Err(FunderGroupingError::Policy(
                        "each normalized reviewed name must occur once".into(),
                    ));
                }
            }
        }
        Ok(policy)
    }
}

/// Builds connected components from exact normalized names and reviewed canonical names.
/// The smallest UUID represents a component, including singletons.
///
/// # Errors
/// Rejects duplicate identities and malformed or ambiguous reviewed policy.
pub fn build_funder_groups(
    funders: &[GroupingFunder],
) -> Result<FunderGroups, FunderGroupingError> {
    let policy = FamilyPolicy::parse(include_str!("funder_families.v1.json"))?;
    let mut ids = BTreeSet::new();
    for funder in funders {
        if !ids.insert(funder.id.value()) {
            return Err(FunderGroupingError::DuplicateFunder(funder.id.value()));
        }
    }
    match policy.version {
        PolicyVersion::V1 => {}
    }
    let mut components = Components::new(funders);
    let mut names = BTreeMap::<NormalizedName, BTreeMap<Uuid, BTreeSet<&str>>>::new();
    for funder in funders {
        for name in std::iter::once(&funder.name).chain(&funder.aliases) {
            if let Some(normalized) = NormalizedName::parse(name) {
                names
                    .entry(normalized)
                    .or_default()
                    .entry(funder.id.value())
                    .or_default()
                    .insert(name);
            }
        }
    }
    let mut alias_links = Vec::new();
    for (normalized, identities) in names {
        let identities: Vec<_> = identities.into_iter().collect();
        for (position, (left_id, left_names)) in identities.iter().enumerate() {
            for (right_id, right_names) in &identities[position + 1..] {
                components.join(*left_id, *right_id);
                for left in left_names {
                    for right in right_names {
                        alias_links.push(FunderAliasLink {
                            left_funder_id: FunderId::new(*left_id),
                            right_funder_id: FunderId::new(*right_id),
                            normalized_alias: normalized.0.clone(),
                            left_alias: (*left).into(),
                            right_alias: (*right).into(),
                        });
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    let mut family_links = Vec::new();
    for family in policy.families {
        let reviewed: BTreeSet<_> = family
            .names
            .iter()
            .filter_map(|name| NormalizedName::parse(name))
            .collect();
        let mut members: Vec<_> = funders
            .iter()
            .filter(|funder| {
                NormalizedName::parse(&funder.name).is_some_and(|name| reviewed.contains(&name))
            })
            .collect();
        members.sort_by_key(|funder| funder.id.value());
        for (position, left) in members.iter().enumerate() {
            for right in &members[position + 1..] {
                components.join(left.id.value(), right.id.value());
                family_links.push(FunderFamilyLink {
                    left_funder_id: left.id,
                    right_funder_id: right.id,
                    family_key: family.key.clone(),
                    left_name: left.name.clone(),
                    right_name: right.name.clone(),
                });
            }
        }
    }
    family_links.sort_by_key(|link| {
        (
            link.family_key.clone(),
            link.left_funder_id.value(),
            link.right_funder_id.value(),
        )
    });
    let memberships = ids
        .iter()
        .map(|id| (FunderId::new(*id), FunderId::new(components.root(*id))))
        .collect();
    Ok(FunderGroups {
        memberships,
        alias_links,
        family_links,
    })
}

struct Components {
    parents: BTreeMap<Uuid, Uuid>,
}

impl Components {
    fn new(funders: &[GroupingFunder]) -> Self {
        Self {
            parents: funders
                .iter()
                .map(|funder| (funder.id.value(), funder.id.value()))
                .collect(),
        }
    }

    fn root(&mut self, id: Uuid) -> Uuid {
        let parent = self.parents[&id];
        if parent == id {
            return id;
        }
        let root = self.root(parent);
        self.parents.insert(id, root);
        root
    }

    fn join(&mut self, left: Uuid, right: Uuid) {
        let left = self.root(left);
        let right = self.root(right);
        self.parents.insert(left.max(right), left.min(right));
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    fn funder(id: u128, name: &str, aliases: &[&str]) -> GroupingFunder {
        GroupingFunder {
            id: FunderId::new(Uuid::from_u128(id)),
            name: name.into(),
            aliases: aliases.iter().map(|name| (*name).into()).collect(),
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn connected_components_keep_direct_evidence_and_split_after_bridge_removal() {
        let mut funders = vec![
            funder(3, "Gamma", &["Bridge two", "ALPHA"]),
            funder(1, "Alpha", &["Bridge one"]),
            funder(2, "Beta", &["  BRIDGE\tONE  ", "Bridge two"]),
            funder(4, "Separate", &["  "]),
        ];
        let groups = build_funder_groups(&funders).unwrap();
        assert_eq!(
            groups
                .memberships
                .iter()
                .map(|(id, group)| (id.value().as_u128(), group.value().as_u128()))
                .collect::<Vec<_>>(),
            [(1, 1), (2, 1), (3, 1), (4, 4)]
        );
        assert_eq!(groups.alias_links.len(), 3);
        assert!(groups.family_links.is_empty());
        funders[0].aliases.clear();
        let groups = build_funder_groups(&funders).unwrap();
        assert_eq!(groups.memberships[2].1.value(), Uuid::from_u128(3));
        assert_eq!(groups.alias_links.len(), 1);
    }

    #[test]
    fn reviewed_policy_rejects_ambiguous_names_empty_keys_and_unknown_versions() {
        for input in [
            r#"{"version":2,"families":[]}"#,
            r#"{"version":1,"families":[{"key":"","names":["BBC"]}]}"#,
            r#"{"version":1,"families":[{"key":"a","names":["BBC"]},{"key":"a","names":["Other"]}]}"#,
            r#"{"version":1,"families":[{"key":"a","names":["BBC"]},{"key":"b","names":[" bBc "]}]}"#,
            r#"{"version":1,"families":[{"key":"a","names":[" "]}]}"#,
            r#"{"version":1,"families":[{"key":"a","names":[]}]}"#,
        ] {
            assert!(FamilyPolicy::parse(input).is_err(), "{input}");
        }
        assert!(matches!(
            build_funder_groups(&[funder(1, "Alpha", &[]), funder(1, "Other", &[])]),
            Err(FunderGroupingError::DuplicateFunder(_))
        ));
    }
}
//! Atomic projection of the pipeline's grouping policy into stored membership and evidence.

use sqlx::{Postgres, Transaction};

use crate::domain::models::{
    entity_ingestion::EntityIngestionError,
    funder::FunderId,
    funder_grouping::{GroupingFunder, build_funder_groups},
};

use super::ExposedDatabase;

impl ExposedDatabase {
    /// Rebuilds grouping for every persisted identity without replaying ingestion artifacts.
    ///
    /// # Errors
    /// Returns database or grouping-policy errors. A failed refresh preserves the previous projection.
    pub async fn refresh_funder_groups(&self) -> Result<usize, EntityIngestionError> {
        let mut tx = self.pool().begin().await.map_err(db_error)?;
        lock_declaration_loader(&mut tx).await?;
        let count = replace_funder_groups(&mut tx).await?;
        tx.commit().await.map_err(db_error)?;
        Ok(count)
    }
}

pub(super) async fn lock_declaration_loader(
    tx: &mut Transaction<'_, Postgres>,
) -> Result<(), EntityIngestionError> {
    sqlx::query!("SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext('exposed.declaration-loader'))")
        .execute(&mut **tx)
        .await
        .map_err(db_error)?;
    Ok(())
}

pub(super) async fn replace_funder_groups(
    tx: &mut Transaction<'_, Postgres>,
) -> Result<usize, EntityIngestionError> {
    let rows = sqlx::query!("SELECT id, funder_name FROM exposed.funders ORDER BY id")
        .fetch_all(&mut **tx)
        .await
        .map_err(db_error)?;
    let aliases = sqlx::query!("SELECT funder_id, funder_alias FROM exposed.funder_aliases ORDER BY funder_id, funder_alias")
        .fetch_all(&mut **tx).await.map_err(db_error)?;
    let mut by_id = std::collections::BTreeMap::<_, Vec<_>>::new();
    for alias in aliases {
        by_id
            .entry(alias.funder_id)
            .or_default()
            .push(alias.funder_alias);
    }
    let funders: Vec<_> = rows
        .into_iter()
        .map(|row| GroupingFunder {
            id: FunderId::new(row.id),
            name: row.funder_name,
            aliases: by_id.remove(&row.id).unwrap_or_default(),
        })
        .collect();
    let groups = build_funder_groups(&funders).map_err(db_error)?;
    sqlx::query!("DELETE FROM exposed.funder_alias_links")
        .execute(&mut **tx)
        .await
        .map_err(db_error)?;
    sqlx::query!("DELETE FROM exposed.funder_family_links")
        .execute(&mut **tx)
        .await
        .map_err(db_error)?;
    sqlx::query!("DELETE FROM exposed.funder_alias_groups")
        .execute(&mut **tx)
        .await
        .map_err(db_error)?;
    for (id, group) in &groups.memberships {
        sqlx::query!(
            "INSERT INTO exposed.funder_alias_groups (funder_id, group_id) VALUES ($1, $2)",
            id.value(),
            group.value()
        )
        .execute(&mut **tx)
        .await
        .map_err(db_error)?;
    }
    for link in groups.alias_links {
        sqlx::query!("INSERT INTO exposed.funder_alias_links (left_funder_id, right_funder_id, normalized_alias, left_alias, right_alias) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5)", link.left_funder_id.value(), link.right_funder_id.value(), link.normalized_alias, link.left_alias, link.right_alias)
            .execute(&mut **tx).await.map_err(db_error)?;
    }
    for link in groups.family_links {
        sqlx::query!("INSERT INTO exposed.funder_family_links (left_funder_id, right_funder_id, family_key, left_name, right_name) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5)", link.left_funder_id.value(), link.right_funder_id.value(), link.family_key, link.left_name, link.right_name)
            .execute(&mut **tx).await.map_err(db_error)?;
    }
    Ok(groups.memberships.len())
}

fn db_error(error: impl std::fmt::Display) -> EntityIngestionError {
    EntityIngestionError::DataError(error.to_string())
}
"}

